# Runtime Rules

`_KDB.runtime` holds technical runtime objects that are not part of dashboard/session state. Runtime is per-session, not serialisable, and never restored from persisted state.

Resources live under their owning module:

```js
_KDB.runtime.<owner>
```

Every owner managing runtime resources also manages their cleanup. Creating a resource without a cleanup path is forbidden.

## What Belongs In `_KDB.runtime`

- live DOM nodes, jQuery objects and host element references;
- DOM listener cleanup records or listener descriptors;
- event-bus unsubscribe handles or subscription cleanup records;
- AbortController instances and their signals;
- observers (MutationObserver, ResizeObserver, IntersectionObserver, custom);
- timeout and interval ids;
- in-flight coordination references for Promises whose underlying operations may need to be aborted on cleanup;
- Cognos API objects and adapter instances;
- transient drag implementation details (pointer position, drag-image node, hover target);
- custom-control runtime nodes registered through module APIs;
- any other per-session technical object that cannot be JSON-serialised.

## What Does Not Belong In `_KDB.runtime`

- selected values, settings and durable user choices (use `_KDB.state`);
- declarative options or labels (use `_KDB.dropdownOptions`, `_KDB.config`);
- Cognos dashboard spec values (use `_KDB.properties`);
- thin host wrappers themselves (use `_KDB.cognosAPI.<theme>.<function>`);
- functions intended to be called from other modules (use `_KDB.api.<module>` or `_KDB.services.<service>`);
- snapshots of state for restore (use `_KDB.state`).

A value that fits in both `_KDB.state` and `_KDB.runtime` is a sign that the value is two values under one name: split it.

## Standard Resource Buckets Per Owner

Use the following bucket names when the resource fits. Do not invent synonyms.

| Bucket | Holds |
|---|---|
| `listeners` | DOM listener cleanup records or listener descriptors |
| `subscriptions` | event-bus unsubscribe handles or subscription cleanup records |
| `abortControllers` | AbortController instances |
| `observers` | MutationObserver, ResizeObserver, IntersectionObserver, etc. |
| `timers` | timeout and interval ids |
| `domRefs` | live DOM nodes or jQuery objects the module mounted itself |
| `adapters` | live adapter instances created at mount time |
| `pendingPromises` | in-flight coordination references; cleanup must abort the underlying operation where possible and ignore stale results |
| `customControls` | per-control runtime registered via custom-control APIs |

Examples:

```js
_KDB.runtime.kdbControl.subscriptions
_KDB.runtime.widgets[widgetId].listeners
_KDB.runtime.header.abortControllers
_KDB.runtime.launchpad.observers
```

A module that needs a bucket not in this list either (a) reuses an existing bucket semantically, or (b) stops and asks for a new bucket name.

## Cleanup Ownership

Every owner cleans up its own runtime resources.

- Module destroy or region cleanup unsubscribes all entries under `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.subscriptions`, removes listeners, aborts controllers, disconnects observers, clears timers, releases DOM references and clears `pendingPromises`.
- Widget cleanup removes everything under `_KDB.runtime.widgets[widgetId].*` before transitioning lifecycle state.
- Bootstrap-owned resources are torn down by bootstrap, not by feature code.

Creating a listener, subscription, observer, timer or controller without a registered cleanup path is forbidden. "We will clean up later" is not a cleanup path.

## AbortController Preference

Where the platform supports it, prefer a single `AbortController` per logical scope over tracking individual handles.

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
_KDB.runtime.kdbControl.abortControllers.main = controller;

document.addEventListener("click", onDocumentClick, { signal: controller.signal });

// On cleanup:
controller.abort();
delete _KDB.runtime.kdbControl.abortControllers.main;
```

Native DOM APIs accept `{ signal }` directly. Whether `_KDB.events.subscribe` accepts a signal depends on the eventBus implementation; when it does not, store the unsubscribe handle in `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.subscriptions` and call it at cleanup. Mixing the two patterns is fine as long as every resource has a cleanup path.

When `AbortController` cannot be used (legacy listener or external API), store the listener descriptor or unsubscribe handle and call the matching `remove*`, `off*` or `unsubscribe` at cleanup.

## Live DOM And jQuery

Live DOM and jQuery references live only in `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.domRefs` or in scoped local variables resolved per render cycle. They do not live in `_KDB.state`, and they do not live in `_KDB.elements` (which holds selectors only).

After a re-render or DOM rebuild, old `domRefs` are stale and must be re-resolved against the new DOM. Do not cache long-lived references to volatile DOM.

## No Serialisation

`_KDB.runtime` is never serialised, persisted or restored:

- not via `JSON.stringify`;
- not via writeback to a Cognos parameter set;
- not via diagnostics dump that is read back as input.

If a value needs to survive a session, it does not belong in `_KDB.runtime`; promote it to `_KDB.state` with a canonical writer.

## Cross-Module Access

Other modules do not read or write into `_KDB.runtime.<owner>`. Runtime is owner-private. Cross-module coordination goes through:

- the owner's public API (`_KDB.api.<owner>` or `_KDB.services.<service>`);
- the event bus (`_KDB.events`);
- shared services that expose deliberate read methods.

Reading another module's runtime to "just see what is going on" is forbidden.

## When AI Must Ask

The AI must explicitly ask the user when at least one of these triggers applies:

- a runtime resource needs a bucket name that is not on the standard list;
- two owners legitimately need to share a runtime resource (rare; usually means one of them is misclassified);
- cleanup ownership is unclear (resource created by module A, lifecycle controlled by module B);
- legacy code stores a runtime object in `_KDB.state.*` and the safe migration target is not obvious.

Ask-template: *"For runtime resource `<X>` I propose placement under `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.<bucket>` because `<reason>`. Acceptable, or should we discuss ownership?"*

## Anti-Goals

- No technical runtime objects in `_KDB.state`.
- No live DOM in `_KDB.elements`; selectors only there.
- No resource created without a matching cleanup path.
- No cross-module access to another module's runtime subtree.
- No serialisation, persistence or restore of `_KDB.runtime`.
- No ad hoc bucket names; use the standard list or ask for a new bucket.

